<<<md>>>
# P1.5 — Speed, Artifacts and Safety (and a map of Pass 2)

**Part:** Pass 1 — The big picture (notebook 5 of 5) · **Reading time:** ~45 min · **Previous:** [P1.4 Contrast and Image Quality](P1.4_Contrast_and_Image_Quality.ipynb) · **Refreshers you may want:** [Fourier properties](../refreshers/RM4_Fourier_Properties.ipynb), [Fourier pairs & sampling](../refreshers/RM5_Fourier_Pairs_and_Sampling.ipynb), [2D Fourier & images](../refreshers/RM6_2D_Fourier_and_Images.ipynb)

**Why this notebook matters.** A basic MRI scan acquires one k-space line per TR, which can take many minutes —
patients move, breathe, and scanners are expensive. Most MRI research of the last 30 years went into going faster:
smarter sequences (EPI, fast spin echo, fast gradient echo), and acquiring *less* data (partial Fourier, parallel
imaging, compressed sensing, deep learning). Every shortcut, and every physical imperfection, produces characteristic
**artifacts** that you must be able to recognise. And because MRI combines a huge magnet, fast-switching gradients and
kilowatts of RF, **safety** is part of every scan. The notebook ends with a map of Pass 2.

<<<md>>>
## Learning goals

1. Compute basic scan time and explain how **FSE/TSE**, **EPI** and **fast gradient echo** shorten it.
2. Explain the principle, gain and price of **partial Fourier**, **parallel imaging** (SENSE/GRAPPA, SMS), **compressed sensing** and **deep-learning** reconstruction.
3. Recognise the main **artifacts** (aliasing, Gibbs ringing, motion ghosts, chemical shift, susceptibility, spike/zipper) and how to reduce them.
4. Name the main **safety** hazards (projectiles, implants, SAR/burns, nerve stimulation, acoustic noise, quench, contrast agents) and their limits.
5. Know where each topic is treated in depth in Pass 2.

<<<md>>>
## Symbols used in this notebook

| Symbol | Meaning (plain words) | SI unit | Typical value / remark |
|---|---|---|---|
| $T_{scan}$ | scan time | s | |
| $TR$, $TE$ | repetition time, echo time | s | |
| $TE_{eff}$ | effective echo time: when the k-space center is acquired in an echo train | s | |
| $N_{TR}$ | number of excitations (TR periods) | – | |
| $N_{PE}$ | number of phase-encode lines | – | 128–512 |
| $NEX$ | number of averages | – | 1–4 |
| $ETL$ | echo train length (k-space lines per excitation) | – | 4–32 (FSE), 64–128 (EPI) |
| $t_{esp}$ | echo spacing (time between echoes in a train) | s | 0.5–1 ms (EPI), 5–15 ms (FSE) |
| $R$ | acceleration (undersampling) factor | – | 2–4 (PI), 4–10 (CS) |
| $g$ | g-factor: extra noise amplification of parallel imaging, $\ge 1$ | – | 1–2 |
| $C_n(\vec r)$ | complex receive sensitivity of coil element $n$ | – | |
| $SNR$ | signal-to-noise ratio | – | $SNR_{acc} = SNR/(g\sqrt R)$ |
| $\vec k$, $k_y$ | k-space position; phase-encode coordinate | m⁻¹ | |
| $FOV$, $\delta$ | field of view; pixel size | m | |
| $BW_{read}$ | receiver bandwidth; per pixel $BW_{read}/N_{read}$ | Hz | |
| $BW_{PE}$ | effective bandwidth per pixel in the EPI phase-encode direction $=1/(N_{PE}\,t_{esp})$ | Hz | 10–30 Hz/pixel |
| $\Delta f$ | off-resonance frequency | Hz | |
| $\Delta f_{cs}$ | chemical-shift offset of fat vs water | Hz | −3.5 ppm ≈ −447 Hz at 3 T |
| $\chi$, $\Delta\chi$ | magnetic susceptibility; difference between two materials | – (ppm) | air–tissue ≈ 9 ppm |
| $\lambda$ | regularization weight (compressed sensing) | – | |
| $B_0$, $dB/dz$ | main field; its spatial gradient (fringe field) | T, T/m | |
| $SAR$ | specific absorption rate | W/kg | 2 W/kg whole body, 3.2 W/kg head |
| $dB/dt$ | rate of change of the gradient field | T/s | PNS threshold ~ tens of T/s |

**Symbol clashes:** $R$ here is the acceleration factor (not resistance, not a rotation matrix); $\lambda$ is a
regularization weight (in P1.2 it was a wavelength).

<<<code>>>
import sys, os
sys.path.insert(0, os.path.abspath(".."))   # make ../mri_tools.py importable
import numpy as np
import matplotlib.pyplot as plt
import mri_tools as mt
mt.setup_plots()
%matplotlib inline

<<<md>>>
## 1. Why MRI is slow

For a conventional sequence (one k-space line per TR):

$$T_{scan} = TR \cdot N_{TR}, \qquad N_{TR} = \frac{N_{PE}\cdot NEX}{ETL\cdot R}.$$

*In words:* scan time = time per excitation × number of excitations. Without tricks ($ETL = R = 1$) we need one TR per
phase-encode line and average. Fast sequences put several lines into one TR ($ETL$ > 1, the **echo train length**);
accelerated methods skip lines ($R$ > 1, the **acceleration factor**).

<<<code>>>
def scan_time(TR, N_PE, NEX=1, ETL=1, R=1):
    """Scan time [s] = TR * N_PE * NEX / (ETL * R)."""
    return TR * N_PE * NEX / (ETL * R)

protocols = [("T2w conventional spin echo",       dict(TR=4.0,   N_PE=256)),
             ("T2w fast spin echo, ETL 16",        dict(TR=4.0,   N_PE=256, ETL=16)),
             ("  ... + parallel imaging R = 2",    dict(TR=4.0,   N_PE=256, ETL=16, R=2)),
             ("T1w spin echo",                     dict(TR=0.5,   N_PE=256)),
             ("spoiled GRE 2D, TR 8 ms",           dict(TR=0.008, N_PE=256)),
             ("single-shot EPI (64 lines, 1 shot)", dict(TR=0.1,  N_PE=64, ETL=64))]
for name, p in protocols:
    t = scan_time(**p)
    print(f"{name:38s}: {t:8.2f} s  ({t/60:5.1f} min)")

<<<md>>>
*Interpretation:* a conventional T2-weighted spin echo would take 17 minutes (for one slice package) — impossible in
the clinic. Echo trains and acceleration bring it to ~1 minute; EPI acquires a whole slice in ~0.1 s.

<<<md>>>
## 2. Fast pulse sequences

**Fast (turbo) spin echo — FSE/TSE/RARE.** After one 90° pulse, a train of 180° refocusing pulses produces $ETL$ spin
echoes, each phase-encoded differently, so $ETL$ lines are acquired per TR. The contrast is set by the **effective
echo time** $TE_{eff}$, when the k-space center is acquired. Price: echoes later in the train have decayed with $T_2$
(slight blurring), the many 180° pulses raise **SAR** (critical at 3 T), and fat appears brighter than in conventional SE.
This is the workhorse for T2-weighted imaging.

![Fast spin echo](../images/pulse_sequence-diagram-fastspinecho-simple.png)

**Echo-planar imaging — EPI.** After one excitation the readout gradient oscillates rapidly while small "blips" of
$G_y$ step to the next line: the zig-zag trajectory of P1.3 covers all of k-space in 30–100 ms. Used for fMRI and
diffusion imaging. Price: the whole echo train lasts tens of ms, so $T_2^*$ decay and off-resonance accumulate along the
phase-encode direction — the bandwidth per pixel in that direction is only $BW_{PE} = 1/(N_{PE}t_{esp})$ ≈ 10–30 Hz,
making EPI very sensitive to distortion and fat shift (Section 4). It is also the loudest, most PNS-limited sequence.

![EPI](../images/pulse_sequence-diagram-EPI.png)

**Fast gradient echo.** No 180° pulses, small flip angles, TR of 2–10 ms. Variants: **spoiled GRE** (FLASH/SPGR,
T1-weighted), **balanced SSFP** (TrueFISP/FIESTA/bFFE, bright fluid, very high SNR, used in cardiac imaging), and
**magnetization-prepared** 3D GRE such as **MPRAGE** (inversion pulse + fast GRE readout, the standard 3D T1-weighted
brain scan).

| ![bSSFP](../images/pulse_sequence-diagram-bSSFP.png) | ![MPRAGE](../images/pulse_sequence-diagram-mprage.png) |
| :-: | :-: |
| balanced SSFP | MPRAGE |

<<<code>>>
# How much off-resonance can each acquisition tolerate? shift [pixels] = Delta f / (bandwidth per pixel)
df_fat = mt.ppm_to_hz(mt.FAT_SHIFT_PPM, 3.0)          # fat-water shift at 3 T [Hz]
cases = {"2DFT readout, 400 Hz/pixel": 400, "2DFT readout, 100 Hz/pixel": 100,
         "EPI phase-encode, 128 lines x 0.5 ms": 1/(128*0.5e-3)}
print(f"fat-water shift at 3 T: {df_fat:.0f} Hz")
for name, bwp in cases.items():
    print(f"{name:38s}: {bwp:6.1f} Hz/pixel -> fat displaced by {abs(df_fat)/bwp:5.1f} pixels")

<<<md>>>
*Interpretation:* in a normal readout fat moves by ~1–4 pixels; in EPI it would move by ~30 pixels, which is why EPI
is always run with fat suppression.

